Rebuild docs/ezr.html as a pycco literate page. Steps:

1. Read ezr.py (do NOT modify it). Write a transformed copy to
   docs/ezr.py: for every top-level "def" line carrying a
   trailing "# comment", move that comment to its own line
   directly above the def; directly above that comment line add
   another comment line holding just the call signature in
   backticks. Example:

       # `add(col: Col, v: Atom, inc=1) -> Col`
       #
       # new Num, or updated Sym; inc=-1 undoes
       def add(col, v, inc=1):

   Signatures carry parameter and return types inferred from
   the `type` aliases at the top of ezr.py (Atom, Col, Row,
   Rows, Tbl, Node) using the code's names-are-types
   convention (tbl: Tbl, col: Col, rows: Rows ...). Params
   with defaults keep the default and get no annotation. Put
   an empty comment line between signature and comment
   text. Defs with no
   trailing comment still get the signature line. Turn section
   banners like "#-- distance ----" into markdown headers
   ("# ## distance"). Strip formfeed characters.
2. Run: pycco -d docs docs/ezr.py    (writes docs/ezr.html).
3. Post-process docs/ezr.html the way the old "make docs" did:
   append etc/custom.css onto docs/pycco.css, and inject
   etc/header.html just after <body>, replacing PROJECT with
   ezr. Keep that header's look: light bar, GitHub and Issues
   links with font-awesome icons, shields badges (python,
   XAI, MIT).
4. Append CSS so prose paragraphs in the docs column are
   justified: .docs p { text-align: justify; }
5. Delete the temporary docs/ezr.py. Leave ezr.py untouched.
6. Open docs/ezr.html and report any def whose comment could
   not be moved.
